# RD(a + b), the largest number of the format not above the exact sum, for every pair of finite
# numbers (milestone2.md, "Rounding a sum down and up"): the rounded sum, one step down when its
# exact error is negative. two_sum_ord's error term is the exact error, so its sign decides. At an
# overflow the error term is the infinity of the opposite sign, which chooses the right
# neighbor: Omega for a sum that overflows upward, -inf for one that overflows downward (the
# format with overflow). An infinite or NaN operand gives the sum of IEEE 754.
kernel:        add_down_1
input:         a
input:         b
output:        r
value:         a+b
ensures:       rounded r down
min_precision: 2
source:        the rounded sum and the sign of its exact error (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
theorem:       proofs/theories/DirectedSum.v add_down_1_correct
status:        proved
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   proved
ieee_theorem:  proofs/theories/IeeeDirected.v add_down_1_ieee_correct
cone_exempt:   r is s or pred(s), chosen by select, and pred(+inf) is finite; the rounded claim states the output for infinite and NaN operands

(s, e) = two_sum_ord(a, b)
n      = lt(e, 0)
p      = pred(s)
r      = select(n, p, s)
